- reflections on the insights I got from my experiences in 2025 which finally concretized this opus. Principally, having to lay down to prostrate in front to the yoruba elders of Ope and Aishats families, and to demonstrate the financial ability to support their marriage through life, Ope and Aishat had to have this wedding, and something felt so complete by the acceptance of the elders of this wedding that the children and their fiends were allowed to dance in joy and exuberance fuelled by alchohol, even when the elders had left, and the elders, assured by what they had seen in the ceremony, never worried about their children's safety, future, and well being as they slept that night (I beleive). the rituals and traditions of african culture refuse to die easily for a reason, and wathcing Aishat, a straight yoruba womna, having the exact same discussions I am having with my parents, that they have closed their minds and stopped presenting authentically as africans.
- reflecions of my experience travelling back from Lagos after my trip, while living out the core principles of this way of life, i.e no matter what, show up for family. no matter the future concenrs, have a smile in your eyes, make sure you see yourself in the person you are talking to and you communicate in such a way that they instantly know you see them as yourself, i.e as a full dignified human being. and when you go to a human gathering always greet everyone and endevour to remember their names, greeting with hugs if possible, and trying in any little way to ease the burdens of thier lives as you know they will also do. most easily by showing 'appreciation', 'tipping them', 'teaching them', or simply providing them companionship through an exchange of songs at the very least, or conversation to acknowldege them before you go back to whatever it is you are doing, or words to encourage their effort if you find them doing work that is beneficial to the community,  if there really is no energy and time at all pata pata to do any of these, never ever forget to greet and exhange the story of your names.